  "worked_solution": "The stationary points of $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ where ${f}^{\u2032}(x)=0.$ Graphically, those are the values where the tangent to the graph is horizontal.\n\nIn the given graph, we can see that\n\n- at any point between $x=-5$ and $x=-2$ the tangent to the graph is horizontal, so we have ${f}^{\u2032}(x)=0$ at those points,\n\n- at any point between $x=2$ and $x=5$ the tangent to the graph is horizontal, so we have ${f}^{\u2032}(x)=0$ at those points,\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980112/assets/6983f3615bf721529173c11de3a9218962a57e4be5247f4bda1ac2fa496531ee.png)\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the $x$-coordinates of the stationary points of $f(x)$ are all values in the intervals $(-5,-2)$ and $(2,5).$ \n\n**Note:** At $x=-2$ and $x=2$, there are sharp corners, so ${f}^{\u2032}(x)$ doesn't exist at those points. This gives us two more critical points, but they are not stationary points.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
